  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 AM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nToday through next Tuesday...A frontal boundary will continue to \nslowly sink south through the forecast area today.  During the \nafternoon hours today, as highs warm into the middle 90s across \nmainly southern portions of the CWA, a stray shower or storm can't \nbe ruled out in the vicinity of this boundary.  The remainder of the \nforecast area will remain dry as highs warm into the low and middle \n90s.\n\nIn the wake of the aforementioned frontal boundary, noticeably drier \nair will filter into the region and remain across the region into at \nleast early next week.  High pressure aloft and at the surface will \nalso assert itself into the region through the beginning of the \nweekend.  Rain chances will essentially be nil across the area \nduring this time.  High temperatures each day will be a bit less \nthan they've been in recent weeks, as they range from the upper 80s \nto low 90s Thursday through Saturday.  Some gradual warming will \nthen ensue Sunday through Tuesday, as highs peak in the low and \nmiddle 90s.  The result of the drier airmass across the region will \nespecially be felt during the night time hours as lows fall into the \n60s.\n\nOnce again, rain chances will remain tough to come by during most of \nthis forecast period.  Ridging aloft will weaken enough on Sunday, \nallowing a disturbance embedded in northwest flow to traverse the \nCWA.  This will bring some isolated showers to the forecast area \nSunday.  Then late Monday into Tuesday, a shortwave dropping \nsoutheast into the region will yield another chance for rain across \nthe forecast area Tuesday into Tuesday night. /19/",
